  "title": "Trump Wants to Meet Kim Again. Would Nuclear Weapons Be on the Table?",
  "deck": "Washington still calls for denuclearization. Kim Jong Un has said talks could resume if the U.S. respects North Korea’s status as a nuclear state.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "news",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-26",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-26",
  "body": [
    "Donald Trump raised the possibility of meeting Kim Jong Un during his Asia trip last week. No meeting took place, and Pyongyang gave no public response to the overture. Days later, North Korean state media reported that Kim had toured nuclear facilities and called for a wider role for tactical nuclear weapons.",
    "Trump has spoken about reopening contact, including by reviving the exchange of letters he and Kim kept up during his first term. He has yet to lay out a public agenda for a new meeting. The dispute that ended their last round of diplomacy remains unresolved. Washington calls for denuclearization, while Kim says the United States would have to respect North Korea’s status as a nuclear state for talks to resume.",
    "## What changed since Singapore",
    "At their first summit in Singapore in June 2018, Trump pledged security guarantees and Kim committed to work toward complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula. The joint statement left the details for later. Their next summit, in Hanoi in February 2019, ended without a deal as the two sides disagreed over sanctions relief and what North Korea would give up in return.",
    "Kim’s public terms today would make it difficult to return to the Singapore formula. He has said talks could resume if Washington drops what he calls its hostile policy and respects North Korea’s nuclear status. His latest comments, reported by state media, described that status as irreversible. The tour’s timing drew attention after Trump’s outreach. North Korea has given no public indication that it scheduled the visit in response to Trump.",
    "## The first decision would be what to negotiate",
    "A meeting could reopen a channel that has yielded little substantive contact since the previous negotiations broke down. The harder question is whether the two governments could agree on a purpose for talks. Denuclearization asks how North Korea would give up its weapons. A discussion focused on reducing tensions could start with narrower measures, such as restoring direct communication, while leaving the arsenal dispute unresolved.",
    "Neither government has publicly announced a new meeting or a joint agenda. The first sign of movement would be a concrete proposal that both sides are willing to discuss."
  ],
